  "step_3": "The decision reason requires identifying outcomes with probability above 1/n and averaging those. Step 1 shows this can be done, and both lotteries yield the same average probable outcome (-500), so the reason would be indifferent. The individual, however, does not mention or appear to calculate averages of outcomes filtered by the 1/n rule. They do consider the possibility of getting 0 in A (which is one of the probable outcomes), and they contrast certainty in B with risk in A — which aligns partially with focusing on high-probability or certain outcomes. However, there is no indication that they applied the specific rule of defining 'probable' as >1/n or computed an average of such outcomes. Their justification is based on loss aversion, risk preference, and personal financial context, not on a mechanical application of the probable-outcome averaging rule. Therefore, while there is superficial overlap (attention to the 0 outcome in A and the certainty in B), the reasoning process does not match the formal structure of the decision reason.",
